  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NP Bhutan TROSA 2.0-BRCS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Bhutan Red Cross Society (BRCS) is a humanitarian organisation and auxiliary to the public authorities, dedicated to improve the collective happiness of the population in all districts by providing emergency, health and social services through its branches
and volunteers.
BRCS&apos;s vision reflects that people in Bhutan, regardless of their age and gender are actively engaged in their local communities for collective happiness and resilience, leaving no one behind.
The Bhutan Red Cross Society foundedin 2017 is determinedto contribute to the successful transition of the Bhutanese society. Based on its mission to improve collective happiness, and inspired by its vision that the people in Bhutan, regardless of their age and gender, actively engage in their communities, the Bhutan Red Cross Society sets the impact goal that by 2030, the communities in Bhutan strengthen their resilience by increasing their ability to prepare for, cope with and recover from disasters and crises, and by improving their health and human welfare whilst leaving noone behind.
Through its branches and volunteers across all districts of the country, the Bhutan Red Cross Society is mobilizing andfacilitating community engagement through an overall program that includes three pillars:
In the disaster resilience pillar, the Bhutan Red Cross Society through its branches complements the efforts of the local district governments to prepare communities for emergency responses in the event of a natural disaster or social crisis. A preparedness for emergency (PER) program promotes contingency planning, early warning and the protection of critical facilities. In case of an emergency, the program triggers early response within the &#x201c;golden&#x201d; 72 hours after an event, and engage in recovery operations. In addition to the preparedness activities, the BhutanRed Cross Society initiated a program that reduces disaster risks and promotes the communities&#x2019; adaptation to climate change. Basedon local risk assessments, Bhutan Red Cross Society branches engages in environmental measures to prevent and mitigate floods, landslides, earthquakes, glacial lake outburst flood (GLOF), and droughts. The success of these programs is dependent on the Bhutan Red Cross Society branches&#x2019; ability to connect with the local government partners, and to mobilize and retain volunteers for community action.
In the community health pillar, the Bhutan Red Cross Society responds to health emergencies such as epidemic outbreaks. As epidemics begin and end in communities, the Bhutan Red Cross Society branches and volunteers plays a key role in the preparedness, surveillance and control of outbreaks. The Bhutan Red Cross Society is implement an emergency health program that mobilizes volunteers to engage in basic emergency health interventions, such as community-based health and first aid (CBHFA) activities and dead body management. At national level, the Bhutan Red Cross Society is expanding its Commercial First Aid program. Following a social enterprise approach, the Bhutan Red Cross Society offers certified high quality first aid trainings to partners in the government, NGO and corporate sectors. A critical condition for the Commercial First Aid program is a major capital investment in a national training facility.
The social inclusion pillar puts the life perspectives of the Bhutanese youth in the center. Two programs on youth engagement and solid waste management is being linked with the aim to develop vocational and life skills of young and disadvantaged men and women in urban settings. Solid waste management activities such as recycling or environmental protection will be initiated to offer young people occupational and income opportunities. These programs will be of innovative and exploratory nature, their success depending on the Bhutan Red Cross Society&#x2019;s social enterprise spirit and good partnerships with civil society organisations and the respective government authorities.</narrative>
  </description>
